These subjective information like patient preferences and feedback, patient motivation, physical and psychological state, and therapists' own work and life experience were among the factors that played crucial role, as well as prioritized, in clinical reasoning of physiotherapists besides just objective data (both motor and physiological). This clinical reasoning helped them set patient goals, decide of treatment plan and adjust rehabilitation sessions (Molle et al., 2025; Holdar et al., 2013). However, novice therapists relied more on informative but objective factors for clinical decision-making compared to experienced therapists, who considered both subjective and objective data to provide personalised rehabilitation (Holdar et al., 2013). This highlights the need to either incorporate or consider subjective data when developing technology for therapeutic process, suggesting therapists should be in the loop instead of adopting full automation (Molle et al., 2025). 

== Therapist Interfaces for Exergame-Based Therapy
Various therapist interfaces for personalized rehabilitation through exergames have been developed, although they do not account for the entirety of the therapy process as they mainly focus on data documentation and/or session planning and task manipulation. One therapist interface for Village Druid VR, a therapy game used for rehabilitation was prototyped along through 2 co-design sessions with 2 therapists each. The lo-fi and hi-fi prototypes focused on patient data documentation for monitoring and progress evaluation as well as some simpler personalization session parameters which were not clearly defined (Ferreira et al., 2022). Similarly, Duval et al. (2022) redesigned an already existing conventional VR game, Spellcasters, through co-design sessions with 14 medical professionals (PT's & OT's). The re-purposed version of Spellcasters focused again solely on data documentation deemed valuable by the medical professionals, and task management through custom rehabilitative gestures to adjust relevant variables including scale, shape, direction and depth.

Another more elaborative study developed a set of collaborative VR serious games, 4 in total, both for upper-limb physical rehabilitation and cognitive rehabilitation of stroke survivors. Again, their framework ADAPT (Adaptive Dynamics for Activity-based Personalized Therapy) only focused on personalized session planning and some game task parameters. For the session planning, therapists could adjust the number of participants, the selection and sequencing of games, session durations and individualized goals. For game task parameters, the therapist could adjust level of difficulty and more detailed adjustments in complexity such as the number and type of objects or puzzle pieces, and in-game payment processes (Figueiredo et al., 2025). 

One study in particular fully isolated game task adjustment in the form of difficulty parameters only. Difficulty in classification in levels were found to lead to longer playtime with more fun and higher performance when compared to high-commitment game parameter goals in terms of goal and time (Eckert et al., 2023). Gagnon-Roy et al. (2024) on the other hand co-developed COOK, a cognitive assistance system together with occupational therapists to help patients regain the ability to prepare meals. Although not an exergame, the patient still has a personalized interface with step-by-step instructions to follow where breaks, reminders and recipe-steps are all pre-configured by the therapist. 

Indisputably, with one exception, previous research does not account for how to share the agency between the therapist and the system. Figueiredo et al. (2025), the exception, highlight in their study that 7 out of 10 participants preferred a hybrid mode combining both automated and manual adjustments, however focused on task personalization only as well. Furthermore, while data documentation does improve evaluation and decision-making and personalized therapy and decision-making, ultimately improving patient care (Tadas et al., 2023; Dawdling et al., 2015 ), the current systems still remain very fragmented as they lack a holistic view of all the main phases in the therapeutic process. The Occupational Intervention Process model includes (1) Evaluation and Goal-Setting Phase, (2) Intervention Phase, (3) Reevaluation Phase (Hinojosa et al., 2017). Physiotherapy largely uses the same basic structure and is often referred to as the "rehab cycle" or "SOAP" which also includes (1) Assessment and goal setting, (2) intervention and (3) re-evaluation (Negrini, 2019; Physiopedia, n.d.; Physiofirstt; 2023). 

