== More-than-Human 

While prior work on therapist interfaces has primarily examined how individual tools support documentation, session planning, and exergame parameter controls, there is a need to look beyond single applications or interfaces. Recent HCI discussions of post-interaction or "fourth wave" present that designers must understand the shifting paradigm of human-computer relations including how agency and responsibility are distributed between people and computational systems (Mensah, 2025). More-than-human design came about as a response to the limitation of human-centered design in addressing complex socio-technical and ecological challenges (Giacardi & Redström, 2020). In a world where it is no longer exclusively humans that act, design, make use, and change, more-than-human design offers a theoretical lens for understanding rehabilitation exergames as an integrated collection of humans (therapists and patients) and non-humans (such as exergame software, algorithms, data representations, sensors, infrastructure), the agency of which are continuously negotiated (Giacardi & Redström, 2020). As these system become more data-driven, healthcare decisions are increasingly influenced by algorithmic logic and historical data apart from just therapist reasoning (source?). This means, in rehabilitation exergames context, the therapy outcomes are shaped not only by therapists' decisions and patients' performance, but also by how algorithms classify performance, how data is presented, what data is shown or hidden, what technology is used, and even institutional constraints. This reiterates the fact that design in therapeutic scenarios are less about optimizing interfaces of users and more about configuring relationships and agencies among multiple human and nonhuman actors over time. 

